# Page History

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

Page History tells readers when a guidance page was first published, what changed and when it last changed. It also holds the review dates for a page, so a reader can judge whether the advice is still looked after.

It is a Civica pattern for guidance pages. Home Assistant has no equivalent. Its History panel and its Logbook record what devices did, which is a different thing. A guidance page can still show a device reading, but that reading keeps its own time.

This page covers:

- Page History
  - Civica Page History Pattern

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

The pattern has three parts, in this order:

1. A heading for the page.
2. The publication line: the date first published and the date last updated, each written in full.
3. A disclosure called “See all updates”. It holds a list of changes, newest first. Each item has a date and a short note saying what changed and what that means for the reader.

A page that has been reviewed also shows the date of its last review and the date its next review is due.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-civica-page-history-pattern"><code>Civica Page History Pattern</code></h3>

The pattern is made from plain HTML: a labelled section, a paragraph with `time` elements, and a native `details` element whose summary is “See all updates”. The list inside is an ordered list, so its order and length are available to assistive technology. Write each date twice: a readable British date as the visible text, and the ISO date in the `datetime` attribute of the `time` element.

The pattern has no theme variables of its own. The Civica theme only affects the surrounding text and lines, through `primary-text-color`, `secondary-text-color` and `divider-color`.

Four kinds of date stay separate:

- **First published.** It never changes.
- **Last updated.** It changes when the content changes in a way that matters to a reader.
- **Last reviewed.** It changes when a person has checked the whole page, even if nothing needed changing.
- **Next review due.** It stays as it is until the review happens. An overdue date is kept and shown, not hidden or moved.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- A history list lets readers see why a page changed, not only that it did.
- The native disclosure works without JavaScript, opens with Enter or Space.
- A closed disclosure hides its list from assistive technology, so keep the latest change in the visible line as well.
- A date does not prove a review. It only records that someone says a review took place.
- The pattern is for guidance pages. It is not a dashboard card, and no theme setting adds it to one.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Show page history on every guidance page. Leave it off the home page and off standalone example pages; the guidance page that owns an example records its changes.

Keep the first publication date fixed. Add the newest change at the top and say what it means: “Corrected the unit in the example from W to kW” helps; “Updated” does not. Do not change every date when the site is rebuilt, and do not imply that unchanged content has been reviewed. Do not change a page’s review date because one sentence changed. A build or release date is not a review.

Keep one date format on every page. Do not show an overdue review by colour alone; say in words that the review is overdue.

Put review dates after the content they cover. The [review workflow](../patterns/review-dates.html) owns what a review checks and when the next one is due.

### Review dates and sensor times

A sensor reading has an observation time: when the device reported it. That time says nothing about whether a person checked the page. Never label a reading “reviewed”, and never show a page date beside a reading as if it applied to the reading. A card that shows a reading needs the meaning of its timestamp, the time zone, and a stale or unavailable state where the integration reports one. Relative text such as “five minutes ago” can sit beside an absolute time but should not replace it. See [Charts and data](../content/formatting-content/charts-and-data.html) and [Status and notifications](status.html).

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.3.1 Info and Relationships: the list, the dates and the disclosure are real elements.
- 2.1.1 Keyboard and 4.1.2 Name, Role, Value: the summary is focusable and opens with Enter or Space.
- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: an overdue review is written, not only coloured.
- 2.4.6 Headings and Labels: the section has a clear label.
- 1.4.10 Reflow and 1.4.12 Text Spacing: notes wrap and stay readable at 320 CSS pixels.

